+# Page Links Carrying a Caller-Supplied Value Use Thymeleaf's Link-Parameter Syntax
+
+**Status:** Accepted
+
+## Context
+
+`REQ-VET-004` keeps the narrowing across pages, so the specialty name the caller supplied is reflected back into every page link of the veterinarian directory.
+
+Every page link in the codebase is built by string preprocessing today. `ownersList.html` writes `@{'/owners?page=__${currentPage - 1}__'}`, pasting the value into the URL text before the link is built. That is safe there because every value those links carry is a page number the application computed. It is not safe for a value an unauthenticated caller chose: `radiology&page=9` pasted into the href smuggles a second parameter into the link.
+
+Thymeleaf's link-parameter syntax, `@{/vets.html(page=${i}, specialty=${specialty})}`, builds the query string from a parameter map instead. Its treatment of a null value is the load-bearing detail, and it is not the intuitive one — see Consequences.
+
+## Options Considered
+
+1. **String preprocessing, matching the owner templates.** One idiom across all templates.
+2. **Link-parameter syntax, one link form for both cases.** Shortest template.
+3. **Link-parameter syntax, with a second link form selected when the directory is unnarrowed** (chosen).
+
+## Decision
+
+A page link carrying a caller-supplied value is built with Thymeleaf's link-parameter syntax. String preprocessing is reserved for links whose every value the application derives.
+
+The vet list template holds two link forms and selects between them on a `narrowed` flag. The unnarrowed form omits the specialty parameter entirely.
+
+Option 1 is rejected because it reflects an attacker-chosen string into an href unencoded. Option 2 is rejected because of the null rendering below. The owner templates are left as they are — this ADR does not migrate them.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Each value is URL-encoded as it enters the query string. `radiology&page=9` renders as `specialty=radiology%26page%3D9`, so it cannot introduce a parameter. A test covers this.
+- Thymeleaf renders a parameter whose value is null as an empty-valued parameter, not by omitting it: `@{/vets.html(page=2, specialty=${specialty})}` with no specialty renders `/vets.html?page=2&specialty=`. Confirmed against Thymeleaf 3.1.5 through `SpringTemplateEngine`.
+- The second link form is what keeps the unnarrowed directory's URLs byte-identical to the ones `REQ-VET-001` served before narrowing existed. The empty-valued parameter would have been harmless on the round trip — a blank specialty already reads as absent — but every unnarrowed page link would have changed.
+- The template carries each link twice, once per form. The pagination block is longer than the owner list's.
+- Two template idioms now coexist. A future author copying the owner pattern into a caller-supplied context reintroduces the unencoded reflection.
+
+## Implementation
+
+**Requirements:** REQ-VET-004
+
+## References
+
+- [Contracts — caller-supplied values in page links](../system-design.md#contracts)
+- [Threat Model — cross-site scripting through rendered user data](../system-design.md#threat-model)
+- [The specialty-narrowed veterinarian read](2026-08-18-specialty-narrowed-veterinarian-read.md)
